//! Structured-concurrency combinators over `future<T>`.
//!
//! This module is Nova's small answer to `Promise.all` / `Promise.race`: a set
//! of `async` helpers that take a group of futures and either wait for all of
//! them, wait for the first, or wait with a timeout. They exist so that
//! application code can express "fan out several awaits and join them" without
//! open-coding the same `await` loop and the same pointer bookkeeping every
//! time.
//!
//! There are two families here, and the split matters:
//!
//!   1. The pure-Nova combinators (`when_all`, `join2`, `join3`, `wait_all`,
//!      `when_first_n`) simply `await` the futures one after another inside a
//!      coroutine. They do NOT run their awaits in parallel: a `future<T>` in
//!      Nova is a handle to work the scheduler is already driving, so awaiting
//!      several of them in sequence still lets all of them make progress
//!      concurrently on the reactor. What these helpers give you is the JOIN
//!      point, the place where all the results are collected, not a separate
//!      parallel launch.
//!
//!   2. The select-style combinators (`selectAny`, `selectTimeout`,
//!      `withTimeout`) genuinely race futures and return as soon as one is
//!      ready, or a deadline elapses. They cannot be written as a plain `await`
//!      loop, because a loop commits to an order and would block on a future
//!      that never completes. Instead they marshal the future handles into a
//!      flat byte buffer of raw pointers and hand that to a runtime intrinsic
//!      (`whenAny` / `whenAnyDeadline`) that parks the coroutine on ALL of them
//!      at once and wakes it on the first edge. The intrinsics are the seam into
//!      the C++ reactor; their Nova bodies here are placeholders that the
//!      compiler and runtime replace, which is why they just `return 0`.
//!
//! The buffer marshalling is deliberate and low-level. A `future<T>` is passed
//! to the reactor as a machine pointer, so each handle is written as an 8-byte
//! slot with [`bytes.write_ptr`] and the address is taken with `as long`. The
//! buffer is always freed with [`bytes.free`] before the combinator returns,
//! including on the await path, so no scratch allocation escapes. Widths are
//! fixed at 8 bytes per slot because these are 64-bit pointers; see the `int`
//! is 32-bit, `long` is 64-bit rule in the language notes.
//!
//! The return values are INDICES, not results. `selectAny` and friends return
//! the position of the future that won (or a negative sentinel for the empty or
//! timed-out case); the caller then awaits or inspects that specific future.
//! This keeps the intrinsic monomorphic over `T`: it only ever moves opaque
//! pointers and reports which one fired.

import list;
import bytes;

/// Runtime intrinsic: park on `n` future pointers packed into `buf` and return
/// the 0-based index of the first one to complete.
///
/// The Nova body here is a placeholder (`return 0`); the real implementation is
/// supplied by the runtime, which reads `n` consecutive 8-byte pointer slots
/// starting at `buf` and blocks the calling coroutine on all of them at once,
/// resolving to whichever future signals first. Call it through [`selectAny`]
/// rather than directly, since that helper owns the buffer marshalling and
/// freeing.
pub fn whenAny(buf: long, n: int): int { return 0; }

/// Waits for the first of `fs` to complete and returns its 0-based index.
///
/// This is Nova's `Promise.race` over a homogeneous list of futures. It packs
/// each future handle into a scratch buffer of 8-byte pointer slots, parks on
/// all of them via [`whenAny`], frees the buffer, and returns the winning
/// index. Returns `-1` if the list is empty, in which case nothing is allocated
/// and nothing is awaited. The result is an index into `fs`, not a value; the
/// caller decides what to do with the winning future.
pub async fn selectAny<T>(fs: List<future<T>>): int {
    let n = fs.size();
    if (n <= 0) { return -1; }
    let buf = bytes.alloc(n * 8);
    let i = 0;
    while (i < n) { bytes.write_ptr(buf, i * 8, fs.at(i) as long); i = i + 1; }
    let idx = await whenAny(buf, n);
    bytes.free(buf);
    return idx;
}

/// Runtime intrinsic: park on `n` future pointers in `buf` with a deadline of
/// `ms` milliseconds, returning the winning index or a timeout sentinel.
///
/// Like [`whenAny`] but with a bound: the runtime replaces this placeholder body
/// (`return 0`) with a park that also arms a one-shot timer, so the coroutine
/// resumes on the first ready future OR when `ms` elapses, whichever comes
/// first. The timeout signalling convention (the sentinel returned when the
/// deadline wins) is defined by the runtime and surfaced by the callers
/// [`selectTimeout`] and [`withTimeout`].
pub fn whenAnyDeadline(buf: long, n: int, ms: int): int { return 0; }

/// Waits for the first of `fs` to complete, but gives up after `ms`
/// milliseconds.
///
/// The bounded variant of [`selectAny`]: it marshals the future handles into a
/// pointer buffer, parks on them all with a deadline via [`whenAnyDeadline`],
/// frees the buffer, and returns the winning index, or the runtime's timeout
/// sentinel if `ms` elapsed first. Returns `-2` for an empty list (distinct
/// from [`selectAny`]'s `-1`, so the two empty-input cases can be told apart),
/// allocating and awaiting nothing in that case.
pub async fn selectTimeout<T>(fs: List<future<T>>, ms: int): int {
    let n = fs.size();
    if (n <= 0) { return -2; }
    let buf = bytes.alloc(n * 8);
    let i = 0;
    while (i < n) { bytes.write_ptr(buf, i * 8, fs.at(i) as long); i = i + 1; }
    let idx = await whenAnyDeadline(buf, n, ms);
    bytes.free(buf);
    return idx;
}

/// Waits for a single future with a deadline of `ms` milliseconds.
///
/// A convenience wrapper over [`whenAnyDeadline`] for the common one-future
/// case: it writes the single handle into an 8-byte buffer, parks on it with
/// the deadline, frees the buffer, and returns the intrinsic's result (index `0`
/// if `fut` completed in time, or the runtime's timeout sentinel otherwise).
/// Prefer this to building a one-element list and calling [`selectTimeout`].
pub async fn withTimeout<T>(fut: future<T>, ms: int): int {
    let buf = bytes.alloc(8);
    bytes.write_ptr(buf, 0, fut as long);
    let idx = await whenAnyDeadline(buf, 1, ms);
    bytes.free(buf);
    return idx;
}

/// Awaits every future in `fs` and collects their results in order.
///
/// This is Nova's `Promise.all`: it returns a new list holding the resolved
/// value of each future, in the same positions as the inputs. The awaits happen
/// sequentially inside this coroutine, but since each `future<T>` is already
/// being driven by the reactor, they still progress concurrently; this function
/// is the join, not a parallel launch. All futures are awaited, so if any never
/// completes this call never returns. Compare [`when_first_n`], which stops
/// after the first `k`, and [`wait_all`], which discards the results.
pub async fn when_all<T>(fs: List<future<T>>): List<T> {
    let out = List<T>();
    let i = 0;
    while (i < fs.size()) {
        out.push(await fs.at(i));
        i = i + 1;
    }
    return out;
}

/// Awaits two futures of possibly different types and returns their results as
/// a pair.
///
/// The heterogeneous counterpart to [`when_all`]: because the two futures carry
/// different element types they cannot go in one `List<future<T>>`, so this
/// takes them positionally and returns a tuple `(A, B)`. The awaits are in order
/// (`fa` then `fb`), which is only a JOIN order and not a scheduling order, both
/// are already running on the reactor.
pub async fn join2<A, B>(fa: future<A>, fb: future<B>): (A, B) {
    let a = await fa;
    let b = await fb;
    return (a, b);
}

/// Awaits three futures of possibly different types and returns their results
/// as a triple.
///
/// The three-way extension of [`join2`], for when three heterogeneous awaits
/// need to be joined at one point. Same ordering note applies: `fa`, `fb`, `fc`
/// are awaited in sequence purely to collect the tuple, all three make progress
/// concurrently under the scheduler.
pub async fn join3<A, B, C>(fa: future<A>, fb: future<B>, fc: future<C>): (A, B, C) {
    let a = await fa;
    let b = await fb;
    let c = await fc;
    return (a, b, c);
}

/// Awaits every future in `fs` for its side effects and returns how many were
/// awaited.
///
/// The fire-and-join variant of [`when_all`] for when the results are not
/// needed, only the completion. Each future is awaited into a throwaway local
/// so the value is released immediately rather than retained in a list, which
/// avoids building an output collection when you only care that the work
/// finished. Returns the count of futures awaited (the size of `fs`).
pub async fn wait_all<T>(fs: List<future<T>>): int {
    let i = 0;
    while (i < fs.size()) {
        let dummy = await fs.at(i);
        i = i + 1;
    }
    return fs.size();
}

/// Awaits the first `k` futures of `fs` (or all of them if `k` exceeds the
/// list size) and returns their results in order.
///
/// A prefix form of [`when_all`]: the count is clamped to `fs.size()` so passing
/// a `k` larger than the list is safe and simply awaits everything. Note the
/// "first `k`" here means the first `k` BY POSITION, not the first `k` to
/// finish; to race for the earliest completions use [`selectAny`] instead. If
/// `k` is zero or negative the returned list is empty and nothing is awaited.
pub async fn when_first_n<T>(fs: List<future<T>>, k: int): List<T> {
    let out = List<T>();
    let n = if (k < fs.size()) k else fs.size();
    let i = 0;
    while (i < n) {
        out.push(await fs.at(i));
        i = i + 1;
    }
    return out;
}
